QB.add(29,[{"id":29001,"t":"Metabolism","s":"William Withering (1789) wrote that “poisons in small doses are the best medicines”. Which idea does this quotation best capture?","o":["The same chemical can be therapeutic or toxic depending on dose","Herbal poisons are always safer than synthetic medicines","Only natural poisons should be used as medicines","Medicines and poisons act through entirely different targets","Toxicity depends on the brand name rather than the dose given"],"e":"A drug is a chemical that affects the functioning of the body, with pharmacological but also toxicological action. The desirable response is efficacy; as the dose gets higher, adverse effects (toxicity) appear. Withering’s line makes exactly this point: dose decides whether a chemical helps or harms.","w":["The lecture made no claim that herbal products are safer.","The quotation is about dose, not about natural sources.","The point is that the same chemical can do both.","Brand names have nothing to do with toxicity; generic names should always be used."],"k":"Dose decides benefit vs toxicity.","rn":[["","Why can polypharmacy cause sub-therapeutic or toxic levels?","Why can polypharmacy cause sub-therapeutic or toxic levels?","Unknown drug interactions"],["","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","Inactive; Less active; Active (therapeutic / toxic effect); Activated (pro-drug)"],["","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","Mode or site of action; Chemical structure; Therapeutic use"]]},{"id":29003,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Which pairing of term and meaning is correct?","o":["Therapeutics – treatment of disease, mostly with medicines","Pharmacology – treatment of disease, mostly with medicines","Therapeutics – the study of drug structure and targets only","Pharmacology – practical application of knowledge to prescribing","Therapeutics – the movement of drugs within the body"],"e":"Pharmacology is the study of drugs (pharma = drug, -ology = study of). Therapeutics is treatment (mostly involving medicines) of disease, knowledge of core drugs and the practical application of that knowledge to prescribing. Movement of drugs within the body is pharmacokinetics.","w":["That is therapeutics; pharmacology is the study of drugs.","Structure and targets are part of pharmacology.","Applying knowledge to prescribing is part of therapeutics.","That defines pharmacokinetics."],"k":"Pharmacology = study of drugs; therapeutics = treatment/prescribing.","rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of therapeutics?","What are the 3 parts of therapeutics?","Treatment of disease (mostly medicines); Knowledge of core drugs; Practical application of knowledge to prescribing"],["","Therapeutics","Therapeutics","Treatment (mostly involving medicines) of disease"]]},{"id":29004,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"A patient with an infection is started on a broad-spectrum antibiotic. A culture and sensitivity test then identifies the bacterium, and the drug is switched to a specific antibiotic. What principle did the lecturer use this to illustrate?","o":["Personalised medicine – the right drug for the right patient","Pharmacokinetics – how the body handles the antibiotic","Enzyme induction – the bacterium speeds up drug metabolism","Rational prescribing of the cheapest drug first","First-pass metabolism of the broad-spectrum drug"],"e":"The lecturer used this example for personalised medicine: you start broad because you don’t know which bacterium is there, then switch to a specific antibiotic once culture and sensitivity identify it. Personalised medicine means the right drug for the right patient at the right dose and time, and much research on it is in pharmacogenomics.","w":["The example was not about absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion.","Nothing in the example involves liver enzymes.","Cost was not mentioned; the point is matching the drug to the patient.","First-pass metabolism is about oral drugs passing the liver."],"k":"Broad → specific after C&S = personalised medicine.","rn":[["","Personalised Medicine","Personalised Medicine","The right drug for the right patient"],["","What does personalised medicine aim to identify?","What does personalised medicine aim to identify?","Right drug; Right patient; Right dose and time"],["","Broad spectrum vs specific antibiotic: which is personalised medicine?","Broad spectrum vs specific antibiotic: which is personalised medicine?","Specific antibiotic; After culture and sensitivity"]]},{"id":29005,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"In studies of adverse drug events, about what proportion are due to medication error?","o":["About 46%","About 39%","About 75%","About 20%","About 80%"],"e":"The study (Sam et al., 2015) found about 46% of adverse drug events were related to medication error (wrong drug, wrong dose, interactions, inadequate monitoring, contraindication, wrong time) and about 39% were adverse drug reactions. The rest included therapeutic failure and overdose.","w":["39% were adverse drug reactions.","75% is the share of drugs metabolised by cytochrome P450 (more than 75%).","20% is the share of a swallowed drug reaching the body in the first-pass figure.","80% relates to first-pass metabolism of oral drugs, not ADEs."],"k":"ADEs: 46% medication error, 39% ADR.","rn":[["","What percentage of adverse drug events were due to medication error?","What percentage of adverse drug events were due to medication error?","About 46%"],["","Which medication errors were the rarest cause of adverse drug events?","Which medication errors were the rarest cause of adverse drug events?","Contraindication (2); Wrong time (2)"],["","How many adverse drug events were medication errors?","How many adverse drug events were medication errors?","71 events; 46.41%"]]},{"id":29007,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Which list gives the six Rights of rational prescribing in order?","o":["Patient, drug, route, dose, time, outcome","Patient, drug, dose, route, time, cost","Drug, dose, route, time, patient, outcome","Patient, diagnosis, drug, dose, time, outcome","Patient, drug, route, dose, frequency, documentation"],"e":"The six Rs are right patient, right drug, right route, right dose, right time and right outcome. They come under rational prescribing and personalised medicine: “right patient leading to right outcome”. Memory aid: Please Don’t Rush Doctors’ Tea Orders.","w":["Cost is not one of the six; dose and route are also swapped.","The list starts with the patient and ends with the outcome.","Diagnosis is not one of the six Rights.","Frequency and documentation are not on the slide."],"k":"6 Rs: patient, drug, route, dose, time, outcome.","rn":[["","What are the 6 Rights of rational prescribing?","What are the 6 Rights of rational prescribing?","Right patient; Right drug; Right route; Right dose; Right time; Right outcome; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":29012,"t":"Development of Pharmacology","s":"Which historical approach to medicine attempted to be science-based but killed many patients?","o":["18th-century allopathy (bloodletting, emetics, purgatives)","19th-century homeopathy (like cures like, dilution)","17th-century remedies of worms, dung, urine and moss","20th-century chemical interactions between drugs and tissues","19th-century combination of therapeutics with physiology"],"e":"The 18th century saw allopathy, an attempt at a science-based approach using bloodletting, emetics and purgatives, and many patients died. Robert Boyle (17th century) recommended worms, dung, urine and moss; homeopathy (19th century) held that like cures like; Ehrlich (20th century) argued for chemical drug–tissue interactions.","w":["Homeopathy relied on like cures like and dilution.","That was Robert Boyle in the 17th century.","That was Paul Ehrlich’s 20th-century view.","That was Rudolph Buchheim’s 19th-century view."],"k":"Allopathy (18th c.): many died.","rn":[["","Allopathy","Allopathy","18th century; Attempt of a science-based approach; Blood letting, emetics and purgatives"]]},{"id":29013,"t":"Development of Pharmacology","s":"Which figure insisted that drug action be understood as conventional chemical interactions between drugs and tissues, not magical “vital” forces?","o":["Paul Ehrlich","Rudolph Buchheim","Robert Boyle","William Withering","William W. Chin"],"e":"In the 20th century Paul Ehrlich insisted drug action should be understood in terms of chemical interactions between drugs and tissues. Buchheim (19th century) said therapeutics must combine with physiology to become a science. Boyle recommended natural remedies; Withering gave the title quotation.","w":["Buchheim argued for combining therapeutics with physiology.","Boyle recommended worms, dung, urine and moss.","Withering is quoted on poisons and medicines.","Chin wrote the quotation on personalised medicine."],"k":"Ehrlich: chemical drug–tissue interactions.","rn":[["","What did Paul Ehrlich (20th century) insist about drug action?","What did Paul Ehrlich (20th century) insist about drug action?","Understood as chemical interactions between drugs and tissues; Not magical \"vital\" forces"]]},{"id":29014,"t":"Development of Pharmacology","s":"What was the key idea of 19th-century homeopathy?","o":["Like cures like, with activity enhanced by dilution","Like cures like, with activity reduced by dilution","Opposites cure, with activity enhanced by dilution","Bloodletting and purgatives remove disease","Drugs act by chemical interactions with tissues"],"e":"Homeopathy (19th century): like cures like, and activity is enhanced by dilution. Allopathy used bloodletting, emetics and purgatives. Ehrlich proposed chemical interactions. The lecture also noted that scientific use of medicines is recent.","w":["The slide says dilution enhances activity in homeopathy.","Homeopathy is “like cures like”.","That is 18th-century allopathy.","That is Ehrlich’s 20th-century view."],"k":"Homeopathy: like cures like; dilution enhances.","rn":[["","Homeopathy","Homeopathy","19th century; Like cures like; Activity enhanced by dilution"]]},{"id":29015,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"Which is the best definition of a drug?","o":["A chemical that affects how the body functions, for good or harm","A chemical licensed as a medicine and prescribed by a doctor","Any substance that cures disease without causing toxicity","A natural plant product with a therapeutic effect","A medicine listed in the BNF with a generic name"],"e":"A drug is a chemical which affects the functioning of the body: medicines, substances of abuse and more. It has pharmacological action but also toxicological action; the effect could be beneficial or harmful. Pharmacology also covers caffeine, nicotine, alcohol, abused substances, food constituents and cosmetics.","w":["Drugs include substances of abuse, caffeine, alcohol and more, not only prescribed medicines.","Drugs also have toxicological action.","Drugs are not limited to plant products.","Many drugs (e.g. caffeine, heroin) are not BNF medicines."],"k":"Drug = chemical affecting body function (good or bad).","rn":[["","Drug","Drug","Chemicals which affect the functioning of the body"]]},{"id":29016,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which of these is NOT listed as something studied in pharmacology?","o":["The surgical techniques used alongside drug treatment","Drug structure, targets and mechanisms of action","Distribution in and handling by the body","Desirable responses (efficacy) and side effects (toxicity)","Caffeine, nicotine, alcohol, vitamins and cosmetics"],"e":"Pharmacology is the study of drugs: structure, targets and mechanisms of action; distribution in and handling by the body; effects including efficacy and toxicity. Drugs include caffeine, nicotine, alcohol, abused substances, food constituents and cosmetics. Surgical technique is not part of it.","w":["This is listed.","This is listed.","This is listed.","These are listed as drugs studied."],"k":"Pharmacology scope.","rn":[["","Why is sublingual used in angina?","Why is sublingual used in angina?","Rapid treatment; Quickly absorbed into systemic circulation"],["","Why does the duration of treatment matter in pharmacokinetics?","Why does the duration of treatment matter in pharmacokinetics?","A drug that stays for 24 to 36 hours; Can cause more adverse effects"],["","What are the 3 parts of therapeutics?","What are the 3 parts of therapeutics?","Treatment of disease (mostly medicines); Knowledge of core drugs; Practical application of knowledge to prescribing"]]},{"id":29017,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"“Anticonvulsant” classifies drugs on which basis?","o":["Therapeutic use","Mode or site of action","Chemical structure","BNF body system number","Generic name ending"],"e":"There is no uniform classification system. Drugs can be classified by mode or site of action (β2-agonists), chemical structure (benzodiazepines) or therapeutic use (anticonvulsant). The BNF classifies by body system, e.g. 3.1.1.1 selective beta2 agonists.","w":["Mode of action example: β2-agonists.","Chemical structure example: benzodiazepines.","The BNF uses system numbers such as 3.1.1.1.","Name endings like -ol are not a classification basis."],"k":"Anticonvulsant = therapeutic use.","rn":[["","Anticonvulsant is an example of which classification?","Anticonvulsant is an example of which classification?","Therapeutic use"],["","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","Mode or site of action; Chemical structure; Therapeutic use"]]},{"id":29018,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"Benzodiazepines (e.g. Diazepam) are grouped by which basis?","o":["Chemical structure","Mode or site of action","Therapeutic use","Route of administration","Brand name"],"e":"The classification triangle gives one example per basis: mode or site of action (β2-agonists), chemical structure (benzodiazepines) and therapeutic use (anticonvulsant). The lecturer gave diazepam as the chemical-structure example.","w":["That example is β2-agonists.","That example is anticonvulsant.","Route is not a classification basis on the slide.","Brand names should not be used."],"k":"Benzodiazepines = chemical structure.","rn":[["","Benzodiazepines are an example of which classification?","Benzodiazepines are an example of which classification?","Chemical structure"]]},{"id":29019,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"The BNF entry 3 → 3.1 → 3.1.1 → 3.1.1.1 leads from “Respiratory system” to which group?","o":["Selective beta2 agonists","Bronchodilators","Adrenoceptor agonists","Anticonvulsants","Benzodiazepines"],"e":"The BNF nests from body system to drug group: 3 Respiratory system → 3.1 Bronchodilators → 3.1.1 Adrenoceptor agonists → 3.1.1.1 Selective beta2 agonists. You will meet the BNF in Year 3.","w":["Bronchodilators are 3.1.","Adrenoceptor agonists are 3.1.1.","Anticonvulsants are not in section 3.","Benzodiazepines are a chemical class, not this entry."],"k":"BNF 3.1.1.1 = selective β2 agonists.","rn":[["","In the BNF, what are 3, 3.1, 3.1.1 and 3.1.1.1?","In the BNF, what are 3, 3.1, 3.1.1 and 3.1.1.1?","3: Respiratory system; 3.1: Bronchodilators; 3.1.1: Adrenoceptor agonists; 3.1.1.1: Selective beta2 agonists"]]},{"id":29020,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"A prescriber writes “Ventolin” on a chart. Write the generic name that should be used instead.","e":"Prescribe by generic name: salbutamol, a selective β2 agonist (“-ol”).","k":"Salbutamol.","a":["salbutamol","albuterol"],"rn":[["","Salbutamol: types of drug name","Salbutamol: types of drug name","Chemical: long systematic name; Generic: salbutamol; Brand: Ventolin"],["","Salbutamol belongs to which drug class?","Salbutamol belongs to which drug class?","Selective β2-agonists"]]},{"id":29021,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"Bambuterol, formoterol, salbutamol and salmeterol share which highlighted feature?","o":["The ending “-ol”, as selective β2-agonists","The ending “-ol”, as benzodiazepines","The prefix “sal-”, as β2-agonists","The same brand manufacturer","The same chemical name"],"e":"The slide lists selective β2-agonists by generic name and highlights the shared ending -ol (bambuterol, formoterol, salbutamol, salmeterol; terbutaline is the exception). You only need to know salbutamol and its function, not all of these names.","w":["They are β2-agonists, not benzodiazepines.","Only two start with “sal-”.","They have many manufacturers.","Each has a different chemical name."],"k":"β2-agonist generics end in -ol.","rn":[["","What ending do most selective β2-agonist names share?","What ending do most selective β2-agonist names share?","-ol"],["","Name 5 selective β2-agonists.","Name 5 selective β2-agonists.","Bambuterol; Formoterol; Salbutamol; Salmeterol; Terbutaline"]]},{"id":29022,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"At which “levels” can pharmacology be studied?","o":["Molecular, (sub)cellular, tissue, whole animal, population","Molecular, (sub)cellular, tissue, organ, organ system and whole body only","Population and whole animal only","Genetic and chemical only","Clinical and pharmacy only"],"e":"Pharmacology is studied at multiple levels: molecular, (sub)cellular, tissue, whole animal or population. It has many subdivisions including pharmacodynamics, pharmacokinetics, pharmacy, toxicology, pharmacogenetics/genomics, medicinal chemistry, clinical and molecular pharmacology.","w":["Whole animal and population levels are also listed.","Molecular to tissue levels are also included.","These are not the levels listed.","These are disciplines, not levels."],"k":"Levels: molecular → population.","rn":[["","At what 5 levels is pharmacology studied?","At what 5 levels is pharmacology studied?","Molecular; (Sub)cellular; Tissue; Whole animal; Population"]]},{"id":29023,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Name the branch of pharmacology describing what a drug does to the body.","e":"Pharmacodynamics: what the drug does to the body; pharmacokinetics: what the body does to the drug (ADME).","k":"Pharmacodynamics.","a":["pharmacodynamics","pd"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 main branches of pharmacology?","What are the 2 main branches of pharmacology?","Pharmacodynamics; Pharmacokinetics"],["","Pharmacodynamics","Pharmacodynamics","What the drug does to the body; Mechanism of action of drugs"]]},{"id":29024,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion describe which branch of pharmacology?","o":["Pharmacokinetics","Pharmacodynamics","Clinical pharmacology","Medicinal chemistry","Molecular pharmacology"],"e":"ADME describes pharmacokinetics: what the body does to the drug, the movement of drugs within the body and the time course of absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion. The lecturer flagged this as a possible AKT-style question.","w":["Pharmacodynamics is the mechanism of action.","Not the branch defined by ADME.","Not the branch defined by ADME.","Not the branch defined by ADME."],"k":"ADME = pharmacokinetics.","rn":[["","Pharmacokinetics is the study of what time course?","Pharmacokinetics is the study of what time course?","Drug absorption (from site of administration); Distribution; Metabolism; Excretion"],["","What are the 4 phases of pharmacokinetics?","What are the 4 phases of pharmacokinetics?","Absorption; Distribution; Metabolism; Excretion; MNEMONIC"],["","Absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion belong to which branch?","Absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion belong to which branch?","Pharmacokinetics"]]},{"id":29025,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which is a correct definition of pharmacokinetics?","o":["The time course of drug ADME","The study of drug targets and mechanisms of action at receptors","The desirable responses to drugs measured in clinical trials","The classification of drugs by therapeutic use","What the drug does to the body"],"e":"Pharmacokinetics is “what the body does to the drug”, “the branch concerned with the movement of drugs within the body” and “the study of the time course of drug absorption (from the site of administration), distribution, metabolism and excretion”. It will be covered in depth in NME.","w":["That is pharmacodynamics.","Efficacy is a drug effect, not PK.","Classification is separate.","That is pharmacodynamics."],"k":"PK = time course of ADME.","rn":[["","Pharmacokinetics is the study of what time course?","Pharmacokinetics is the study of what time course?","Drug absorption (from site of administration); Distribution; Metabolism; Excretion"]]},{"id":29026,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"Which of the following is NOT one of the reasons for why pharmacokinetics is relevant?","o":["The chemical structure of the drug","Lifetime (half-life, t1/2)","Dosage regimen and dose adjustment","Interactions","Route of administration and duration of treatment"],"e":"The slide links PK to lifetime (t1/2), dosage regimen, dose adjustment, interactions, route of administration, dose and duration of treatment. Chemical structure was not on this list (and structures are not required for core drugs).","w":["Half-life is listed.","These are listed.","Interactions are listed.","These are listed."],"k":"PK relevance list.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","Mode or site of action; Chemical structure; Therapeutic use"],["","Benzodiazepines are an example of which classification?","Benzodiazepines are an example of which classification?","Chemical structure"]]},{"id":29027,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"A drug stays in the body for 24–36 hours. Why does this matter for prescribing?","o":["It could cause more adverse effects","It means the drug cannot be given orally and must be given by injection","It shows the drug is a pro-drug","It means the drug avoids first-pass metabolism","It means it must be given IV"],"e":"You need to know the dose regimen and duration required: if a drug stays in the body a long time (24 or 36 hours) it could cause more adverse effects. This is why lifetime (t1/2), regimen and duration are part of PK’s relevance.","w":["A long stay matters for adverse effects, regimen and duration.","Pro-drugs need metabolic activation; half-life does not show this.","First-pass depends on route.","Route is decided by other factors."],"k":"Long stay → more adverse effects.","rn":[["","Why does the duration of treatment matter in pharmacokinetics?","Why does the duration of treatment matter in pharmacokinetics?","A drug that stays for 24 to 36 hours; Can cause more adverse effects"]]},{"id":29028,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which statement about the oral route is correct?","o":["Most common; convenient, acceptable, no special skills","Most common; fastest route in an emergency","Least common; avoids first-pass metabolism","Most common; goes straight to the systemic circulation","Least common; needs a cannula"],"e":"Oral (p.o., per os) is the most common route: convenient, acceptable, no special skills needed, and many pharmaceutical forms for different patients. However, oral drugs pass through first-pass hepatic metabolism and may take about an hour to reach the systemic circulation, so IV is used in emergencies.","w":["IV is preferred in an emergency.","Oral is the most common and undergoes first pass.","Oral drugs pass the liver first.","IV needs a cannula."],"k":"Oral: commonest, convenient.","rn":[["","What are the 4 advantages of oral administration?","What are the 4 advantages of oral administration?","Convenient; Acceptable; No special skills; Variety of pharmaceutical solutions for different patients"]]},{"id":29029,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"A patient with angina needs rapid relief from a GTN tablet placed under the tongue. Name this route.","e":"Sublingual absorption enters the systemic circulation directly, bypassing first-pass metabolism.","k":"Sublingual.","a":["sublingual","sublingually","under the tongue"],"rn":[["","Which route avoids first-pass metabolism and is placed under the tongue?","Which route avoids first-pass metabolism and is placed under the tongue?","Sublingual"]]},{"id":29030,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"A patient is unconscious and cannot swallow. Which route is an example for this situation?","o":["Rectal (e.g. suppository)","Oral, crushed and mixed into a drink","Sublingual","Inhalation","Buccal"],"e":"Rectal administration, e.g. a suppository, was given for when the patient is unconscious. “If the patient is unconscious you cannot give drugs orally.” Sublingual/buccal routes are for urgent needs; inhalation is for rapid action (e.g. salbutamol in asthma).","w":["An unconscious patient cannot take drugs orally.","This needs the patient to hold the drug under the tongue.","Inhalation was linked to asthma.","Buccal needs the drug held in the mouth."],"k":"Unconscious → rectal.","rn":[["","What is an example of a rectal preparation?","What is an example of a rectal preparation?","Suppository"],["","When could rectal or non-oral routes be needed?","When could rectal or non-oral routes be needed?","Patient unconscious; Patient vomiting (cannot take orally)"]]},{"id":29031,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Salbutamol for asthma is given by which route?","e":"Inhalation delivers drug to the airways.","k":"Inhalation.","a":["inhalation","inhaled","inhaler","inhalational"],"rn":[["","Inhalation is used for drugs for which condition?","Inhalation is used for drugs for which condition?","Asthma; e.g. salbutamol"]]},{"id":29032,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which statement about IV administration is correct?","o":["Avoid IV unless absolutely necessary","Use IV first, as it is the most convenient and acceptable route","IV is safest because no special skills are needed","IV passes through the liver before acting","IV is used only for rectal-type emergencies"],"e":"“We do not go for IV unless it is absolutely necessary.” If the patient can take it orally there is no point giving IV, unless there is, e.g., a serious infection. In an emergency IV is better because it goes directly into the systemic circulation.","w":["Oral is the convenient route; IV only if necessary.","Oral needs no special skills.","IV avoids the liver.","Not."],"k":"IV only if necessary.","rn":[["","When should IV be used instead of oral?","When should IV be used instead of oral?","Only when absolutely necessary; e.g. serious infection"]]},{"id":29033,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"Which definition of absorption is correct?","o":["Transfer from the site of administration into the systemic circulation","Transfer of a drug from the systemic circulation into the body’s tissues and fat","Chemical alteration of the drug to aid its elimination","Removal of the drug or its metabolites from the body","Binding of the drug to albumin"],"e":"Absorption is the process of transfer of the drug from the site of administration into the general/systemic circulation; it influences access to the systemic circulation. Transfer into tissues is distribution; chemical alteration is metabolism; removal is excretion.","w":["That is distribution.","That is metabolism.","That is excretion.","Protein binding is part of distribution."],"k":"Absorption: site → systemic circulation.","rn":[["","Absorption","Absorption","Transfer of the drug from the site of administration into the general / systemic circulation"]]},{"id":29034,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","f":"ph24","s":"Which sequence does a swallowed drug follow before reaching the systemic circulation?","o":["Gut enzymes → hepatic portal system → liver → rest of body","Gut enzymes → liver → hepatic portal system → rest of body","Hepatic portal system → gut → liver → rest of body","Liver → gut enzymes → systemic circulation → portal vein","Gut → systemic circulation → liver → rest of body"],"e":"Swallowed drug (100%) → digestive system (enzymes) → hepatic portal system → liver (enzymes) → rest of the body (20%). IV injection enters the systemic circulation directly. The lecturer said 75–80% of orally given drugs go through this first-pass hepatic metabolism.","w":["The portal system carries drug to the liver, so it comes before it.","The drug is absorbed from the gut first.","Not.","The liver comes before the systemic circulation."],"k":"Gut → portal → liver → body.","rn":[["","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","Digestive system (enzymes); Hepatic portal system; Liver (enzymes); Rest of the body"]]},{"id":29035,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","f":"ph24","s":"In a drug with heavy first-pass metabolism, what proportion of a swallowed dose might reach the rest of the body?","o":["20%","100%","80%","50%","75%"],"e":"The figure shows 100% swallowed and 20% reaching the rest of the body after gut and liver enzymes. IV avoids this, because the drug enters directly into the systemic circulation.","w":["100% is what is swallowed.","About 75–80% of oral drugs undergo first pass; 20% reaches the body here.","Not the figure value.","Not the figure value."],"k":"100% in → 20% out.","rn":[["","If 100% of a drug is swallowed, how much reaches the rest of the body?","If 100% of a drug is swallowed, how much reaches the rest of the body?","20%"]]},{"id":29036,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"Name the effect by which a swallowed drug is extensively metabolised by the liver before reaching the systemic circulation.","e":"First-pass metabolism: gut enzymes → hepatic portal system → liver → rest of body; overcome by IV or sublingual routes.","k":"First-pass metabolism.","a":["first-pass metabolism","first pass metabolism","first-pass effect","first pass effect","first pass","first-pass"],"rn":[["","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","Digestive system (enzymes); Hepatic portal system; Liver (enzymes); Rest of the body"],["","First-pass Metabolism","First-pass Metabolism","Oral drugs pass through the liver before the systemic circulation"],["","How do you overcome first-pass metabolism?","How do you overcome first-pass metabolism?","IV injection"]]},{"id":29037,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"In a serious emergency, why is IV preferred over oral?","o":["IV bypasses the liver, so it acts very quickly","IV is metabolised faster by the liver, so it acts quickly","IV is absorbed through the stomach faster","Oral drugs cannot reach the systemic circulation at all","IV drugs bind less to albumin"],"e":"His reason: IV does not go into the liver; it goes directly into the systemic circulation, so the effect is very quick. Orally it might take about one hour to reach the systemic circulation. A student also noted the patient may be unable to swallow.","w":["IV avoids the liver first.","IV is not absorbed from the stomach.","Oral drugs do reach it, but slowly and reduced.","Not the reason given."],"k":"IV skips liver → fast."},{"id":29038,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"About what percentage of orally given drugs undergo first-pass hepatic metabolism?","o":["75–80%","20%","46%","100%","39%"],"e":"He said 75–80% of orally given drugs go through first-pass hepatic metabolism. The figure example shows 100% swallowed and 20% reaching the rest of the body.","w":["20% is what reaches the body in the figure.","46% is ADEs from medication error.","Not all.","39% is ADRs."],"k":"75–80% of oral drugs undergo first pass.","rn":[["","About what percentage of oral drugs go through first-pass metabolism?","About what percentage of oral drugs go through first-pass metabolism?","75 to 80%"]]},{"id":29039,"t":"Distribution","s":"Which definition of distribution is correct?","o":["Transfer from the general circulation to the body and tissues","Transfer from the site of administration to the circulation","Chemical change that enhances elimination","Removal by the kidney","Binding to cytochrome P450"],"e":"Distribution is the process by which a compound is transferred from the general circulation to other parts of the body and into the tissues – where the drug goes, depending on its characteristics.","w":["That is absorption.","That is metabolism.","That is excretion.","P450 enzymes metabolise drugs."],"k":"Distribution: circulation → tissues.","rn":[["","Distribution","Distribution","Transfer of a compound from the general circulation to other parts of the body and into the tissues"]]},{"id":29040,"t":"Distribution","s":"Diazepam is highly lipid-soluble. In which body compartment would most of it be found?","e":"Lipid-soluble drugs (diazepam) accumulate in fat; heparin stays in plasma; mannitol in extracellular water; ethanol in total body water; fluoride in bone and teeth.","k":"Adipose tissue.","a":["adipose","adipose tissue","fat","body fat","fat tissue"],"rn":[["","What are the 5 distribution compartments?","What are the 5 distribution compartments?","Total body water; Extracellular water; Blood plasma; Adipose tissue; Bone and teeth"],["","Diazepam","Diazepam","Adipose tissue"],["","Which molecules distribute into adipose tissue?","Which molecules distribute into adipose tissue?","Highly lipid-soluble molecules"]]},{"id":29041,"t":"Distribution","s":"Heparin is a very large, highly charged molecule. Which compartment does it mainly occupy?","e":"Heparin is confined to plasma because of its size and charge.","k":"Plasma.","a":["plasma","blood plasma","blood","intravascular"],"rn":[["","Heparin","Heparin","Blood plasma"],["","Which molecules stay in blood plasma?","Which molecules stay in blood plasma?","Highly plasma protein-bound; Very large; Highly charged"]]},{"id":29042,"t":"Distribution","s":"Which pairing of compartment and example drug is correct?","o":["Extracellular water – mannitol","Total body water – mannitol","Extracellular water – ethanol","Adipose tissue – fluoride","Bone and teeth – diazepam"],"e":"Total body water: ethanol; extracellular water: mannitol; plasma: heparin; adipose: diazepam; bone and teeth: fluoride.","w":["Total body water example is ethanol.","Ethanol is in total body water.","Fluoride goes to bone and teeth.","Diazepam goes to adipose."],"k":"Mannitol → extracellular water.","rn":[["","Mannitol","Mannitol","Extracellular water"]]},{"id":29043,"t":"Distribution","s":"What role does albumin play in drug distribution?","o":["A reservoir that releases bound drug when needed","An enzyme that breaks down most drugs in the blood","A carrier that removes drugs into the urine","A barrier that keeps drugs out of the plasma","A receptor that produces the drug’s effect"],"e":"Most drugs bind to proteins, especially albumin. Albumin acts as a reservoir: drugs in the blood bind to it, and whenever the body or cells need the drug, albumin releases it.","w":["Metabolism is mainly by liver P450 enzymes.","Excretion is mainly renal.","Albumin is in plasma and holds drug there.","Not."],"k":"Albumin = drug reservoir.","rn":[["","How does albumin act as a reservoir?","How does albumin act as a reservoir?","Binds the drug in blood; Releases it when cells need it"]]},{"id":29044,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Which definition of metabolism is correct?","o":["Chemical alteration that aids action or elimination","Chemical alteration that always inactivates the drug before excretion","Transfer of the drug into tissues","Removal of the drug from the body","Absorption of the drug from the gut"],"e":"Metabolism: the processes by which a drug is chemically altered in a way that facilitates its action OR enhances its elimination. The metabolite can be inactive, less active, active (therapeutic/toxic) or activated (pro-drug).","w":["It can also activate a drug (pro-drug).","That is distribution.","That is excretion.","That is absorption."],"k":"Metabolism: alters for action or elimination.","rn":[["","Metabolism","Metabolism","Processes by which a drug is chemically altered; Facilitates its action OR enhances its elimination"]]},{"id":29045,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Which is NOT one of the possible outcomes for a metabolite?","o":["Converted back into the original drug","Inactive","Less active","Active (therapeutic/toxic effect)","Activated (pro-drug)"],"e":"The metabolite can be inactive, less active, active (therapeutic or toxic effect) or activated (from a pro-drug). Conversion back into the original drug is not listed.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Metabolite outcomes."},{"id":29046,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A drug is given in an inactive form and becomes active only after liver metabolism. What is it called?","e":"A pro-drug is activated by metabolism; an enzyme inducer would increase its activation.","k":"Pro-drug.","a":["pro-drug","prodrug","pro drug"],"rn":[["","Where is a pro-drug changed into its active form?","Where is a pro-drug changed into its active form?","The liver"],["","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","Inactive; Less active; Active (therapeutic / toxic effect); Activated (pro-drug)"]]},{"id":29047,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Name the major organ of drug metabolism (about 70–80%).","e":"The liver is the main site; kidney, gut, lungs and skin also metabolise drugs.","k":"Liver.","a":["liver","hepatic"],"rn":[["","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","Liver; Kidney; GI tract; Lungs; Skin"],["","About what percentage of drug metabolism happens in the liver?","About what percentage of drug metabolism happens in the liver?","70 to 80%"]]},{"id":29048,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Name the liver enzyme superfamily that metabolises over 75% of drugs.","e":"Cytochrome P450: inhibitors raise levels of co-administered drugs (toxicity); inducers lower them (sub-therapeutic).","k":"Cytochrome P450.","a":["cytochrome p450","p450","cyp450","cyp","cytochrome p-450","cyp p450"],"rn":[["","CP450","CP450","Cytochrome P450"],["","Cytochrome P450","Cytochrome P450","Superfamily of enzymes; Metabolise more than 75% of drugs used in medicine"]]},{"id":29049,"t":"Metabolism","f":"ph27","s":"Drug A is metabolised by liver P450. Drug B, a P450 inhibitor, is added. What happens to drug A's plasma level?","o":["It rises towards the toxicity line","It falls towards the sub-therapeutic line","It stays between the two lines","It rises, then falls below sub-therapeutic","It disappears from plasma"],"e":"An inhibitor blocks the enzyme that metabolises drug A, so metabolism is slower and its level goes higher, towards toxicity. An inducer (drug C) makes more enzyme, so drug A falls towards sub-therapeutic.","w":["That is the inducer (drug C).","That is drug A alone.","Not shown.","Not."],"k":"Inhibitor → ↑ level → toxicity."},{"id":29050,"t":"Metabolism","f":"ph27","s":"Drug C, an enzyme inducer, is given with drug A. Which risk results?","o":["A sub-therapeutic level of drug A","A toxic level of drug A from slower metabolism","No change in drug A","Drug A becoming a pro-drug","Drug A avoiding first pass"],"e":"With an inducer there are more enzymes, so more of drug A is metabolised and its level falls towards the sub-therapeutic line – risk of treatment failure. With an inhibitor the level rises towards toxicity.","w":["An inducer speeds metabolism.","It falls.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inducer → ↓ level → sub-therapeutic.","rn":[["","What is the risk of giving drug A with an enzyme inducer?","What is the risk of giving drug A with an enzyme inducer?","Sub-therapeutic levels"]]},{"id":29051,"t":"Metabolism","s":"In the induction vs inhibition table, which row is correct for enzymatic INDUCTION?","o":["Therapeutic effect ↓, toxicity ↓, first-pass ↑, metabolism ↑","Therapeutic effect ↑, toxicity ↑, first-pass ↓, metabolism ↓","Therapeutic effect ↓, toxicity ↑, first-pass ↑, metabolism ↑","Therapeutic effect ↑, toxicity ↓, first-pass ↑, metabolism ↓","No change in any"],"e":"Induction: more P450, more breakdown, lower drug level – therapeutic effect ↓, toxicity ↓, first-pass metabolism ↑, metabolism rate ↑. Inhibition has the opposite effects.","w":["That is inhibition.","Toxicity falls with induction.","Therapeutic effect falls; metabolism rises.","It changes."],"k":"Induction: effect ↓ toxicity ↓ first-pass ↑ rate ↑.","rn":[["","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Therapeutic effect: down; Toxicity: down; First pass metabolism: up; Metabolism rate: up"],["","Enzyme inhibition: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Enzyme inhibition: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Therapeutic effect: up; Toxicity: up; First pass metabolism: down; Metabolism rate: down"]]},{"id":29052,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Which factors can cause enzymatic induction or inhibition?","o":["Genes, environment, diet, drugs, medical conditions","Only other drugs given at the same time, never diet or genetics","Only genetic factors","Only the route of administration","Only the patient’s age"],"e":"Induction and inhibition are influenced by genetic and environmental factors, diet, drugs and medical conditions – not just the drug itself.","w":["Genetic, environmental, dietary and disease factors also count.","Not only genetics.","Not listed.","Not listed."],"k":"Causes: genes, environment, diet, drugs, disease.","rn":[["","What 3 factors cause enzyme inhibition and induction?","What 3 factors cause enzyme inhibition and induction?","Genetic and environmental factors; Diet, drugs; Medical conditions"]]},{"id":29053,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A drug normally given as 100 mg acts like 50 mg in one patient. What could cause this?","o":["More enzymes breaking the drug down (induction)","Fewer enzymes breaking the drug down (inhibition)","The drug being a pro-drug","The drug avoiding first-pass metabolism","Albumin releasing more drug"],"e":"With induction more enzymes break the drug down, so the effective dose drops (100 mg acting like 50 mg) and treatment is less effective. The drug’s name was not clear on the recording.","w":["Inhibition raises levels.","Not the example.","That would raise levels.","Not."],"k":"Induction lowers effective dose.","rn":[["","Why does an enzyme inducer lower drug A levels?","Why does an enzyme inducer lower drug A levels?","More enzymes; More metabolism of drug A"],["","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Therapeutic effect: down; Toxicity: down; First pass metabolism: up; Metabolism rate: up"]]},{"id":29054,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Why must clinicians know drug interactions involving enzyme inducers and inhibitors?","o":["With polypharmacy, levels can suddenly become toxic or sub-therapeutic","Because inducers and inhibitors are always given together deliberately","Because interactions only matter for pro-drugs","Because inhibitors stop absorption from the gut","Because inducers convert drugs into brand names"],"e":"You would not deliberately combine these; as a clinician you should know the interactions. With polypharmacy, if you don’t know them, reactions can suddenly happen – that is how sub-therapeutic and toxic levels occur.","w":["He said you would not do this deliberately.","They matter for all metabolised drugs.","Inhibitors slow metabolism.","Not."],"k":"Polypharmacy → interaction risk.","rn":[["","Why can polypharmacy cause sub-therapeutic or toxic levels?","Why can polypharmacy cause sub-therapeutic or toxic levels?","Unknown drug interactions"]]},{"id":29055,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Name the main organ (route) of drug excretion.","e":"Renal excretion is the main route, so renal function matters for most drugs.","k":"Kidney (renal).","a":["kidney","kidneys","renal","urine","renal excretion"],"rn":[["","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","Liver; Kidney; GI tract; Lungs; Skin"]]},{"id":29056,"t":"Excretion","s":"Why are hair and nails of interest in drug excretion?","o":["Forensic toxicology, e.g. arsenic deposited in them","They are the main route of excretion for most drugs","They metabolise drugs by cytochrome P450","They act as a reservoir like albumin","They avoid first-pass metabolism"],"e":"Some drugs are excreted into hair and nails. In forensic toxicology, at autopsy, hair and nails are analysed – e.g. in arsenic poisoning, arsenic is deposited in hair and nails.","w":["Renal is the main route.","Not.","Albumin is the reservoir.","Not."],"k":"Hair/nails: forensic (arsenic).","rn":[["","Which poison is deposited in the nails and hair?","Which poison is deposited in the nails and hair?","Arsenic"],["","What is the importance of drugs excreted in hair and nails?","What is the importance of drugs excreted in hair and nails?","Forensic toxicology"]]},{"id":29057,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which ordering reflects ADME?","o":["Absorption → distribution → metabolism → excretion","Administration → digestion → metabolism → elimination","Absorption → digestion → modification → excretion","Distribution → absorption → metabolism → excretion","Absorption → metabolism → distribution → excretion"],"e":"ADME = absorption (A can also be administration), distribution, metabolism and excretion – the time course of the drug in the body (pharmacokinetics).","w":["D is distribution.","D is distribution; M is metabolism.","Absorption comes first.","Distribution comes before metabolism."],"k":"ADME order.","rn":[["","ADME","ADME","Absorption (or Administration), Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion"],["","Pharmacokinetics is the study of what time course?","Pharmacokinetics is the study of what time course?","Drug absorption (from site of administration); Distribution; Metabolism; Excretion"],["","What are the 4 phases of pharmacokinetics?","What are the 4 phases of pharmacokinetics?","Absorption; Distribution; Metabolism; Excretion; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":29061,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"A drug given orally has a much weaker effect than the same dose given IV. Which explanation fits best?","o":["Oral drug is reduced by first-pass gut and liver metabolism","IV drug is bound to albumin, so more of it reaches tissues","Oral drug is excreted by the kidney before absorption","IV drug is a pro-drug activated in the blood","Oral drug is distributed only into bone and teeth"],"e":"An oral drug passes the digestive system enzymes, the hepatic portal system and the liver before reaching the rest of the body (100% → 20% in the figure). IV goes directly into the systemic circulation, so the full dose is available. This is first-pass metabolism.","w":["Albumin binding is not the reason IV is stronger.","Excretion comes after absorption and distribution.","Nothing suggests a pro-drug; route explains it.","Bone and teeth take certain ions like fluoride."],"k":"Oral weaker than IV: first pass.","rn":[["","First-pass Metabolism","First-pass Metabolism","Oral drugs pass through the liver before the systemic circulation"],["","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","Digestive system (enzymes); Hepatic portal system; Liver (enzymes); Rest of the body"]]},{"id":29062,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A patient on drug A starts a second medicine that inhibits cytochrome P450. Two weeks later they develop side effects from drug A. What is the most likely mechanism?","o":["Slower metabolism of A, raising its level towards toxicity","Faster metabolism of A, lowering its level below therapeutic","Greater first-pass metabolism of A, lowering its level","Albumin releasing A into the urine","Conversion of A into an inactive brand"],"e":"An inhibitor slows metabolism, so drug A’s plasma level rises towards the toxicity line. Inhibition increases therapeutic effect and toxicity and decreases first-pass metabolism and metabolism rate.","w":["That is induction.","Inhibition decreases first pass.","Albumin is a reservoir; excretion is renal.","Not."],"k":"Inhibitor → toxicity.","rn":[["","Enzyme inhibition: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Enzyme inhibition: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Therapeutic effect: up; Toxicity: up; First pass metabolism: down; Metabolism rate: down"],["","Why does an enzyme inhibitor raise drug A levels?","Why does an enzyme inhibitor raise drug A levels?","Less metabolism of drug A"],["","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Therapeutic effect: down; Toxicity: down; First pass metabolism: up; Metabolism rate: up"]]},{"id":29063,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A patient stable on drug A starts a P450 inducer and their condition worsens. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["A is metabolised faster, becoming sub-therapeutic","Drug A is metabolised slower, rising to toxic levels in the plasma","Drug A is now excreted only in hair and nails","Drug A no longer binds to its receptor","Drug A is now absorbed sublingually"],"e":"An inducer increases enzyme amount, so more drug A is metabolised and its level falls towards sub-therapeutic – treatment failure. Induction: therapeutic effect ↓, toxicity ↓, first-pass ↑, metabolism rate ↑.","w":["That is inhibition.","Not.","That is pharmacodynamics; nothing suggests it.","Route does not change."],"k":"Inducer → sub-therapeutic.","rn":[["","What is the risk of giving drug A with an enzyme inducer?","What is the risk of giving drug A with an enzyme inducer?","Sub-therapeutic levels"]]},{"id":29064,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A drug is a pro-drug activated by liver P450. Using the definitions, what would an enzyme INDUCER be expected to do to the amount of active drug formed?","o":["Increase it, as more pro-drug is metabolised","Decrease it, as more active drug is destroyed","Have no effect, as pro-drugs bypass P450","Decrease it, as the pro-drug is excreted renally","Stop it completely"],"e":"A pro-drug must be metabolised in vivo into the active drug. Induction means faster metabolism, so more pro-drug would be converted to active drug. The slide posed “What if it is a prodrug?” – the answer follows from the pro-drug definition and the induction row of the table. (The lecturer’s spoken answer was garbled on the recording.)","w":["For a pro-drug, metabolism forms the active drug.","Pro-drugs need metabolic activation.","Not given.","Not."],"k":"Pro-drug + inducer → more active drug.","rn":[["","Pro-drug","Pro-drug","Precursor of the active drug; Needs to be metabolised (in vivo) into active drugs"]]},{"id":29065,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Two patients take the same dose of the same drug but one has a much higher plasma level. Which factor could explain this?","o":["Genetic or environmental differences in enzymes","Different brand names of the same generic drug changing its potency","Different BNF classification numbers","The drug being a β2-agonist in one patient only","Different chemical names on the label"],"e":"Enzymatic induction and inhibition are influenced by genetic and environmental factors, diet, drugs and medical conditions. These change metabolism and so plasma levels. Every human being is different, which is why pharmacogenomics research into personalised medicine matters.","w":["The generic drug is the same.","Classification does not change levels.","Same drug, same class.","Not."],"k":"Individual variation: genes/environment.","rn":[["","What 3 factors cause enzyme inhibition and induction?","What 3 factors cause enzyme inhibition and induction?","Genetic and environmental factors; Diet, drugs; Medical conditions"]]},{"id":29066,"t":"Distribution","s":"A drug is highly lipid-soluble. Where would most of a dose go?","o":["Adipose tissue","Total body water","Divided equally","Neither – it stays in plasma","Bone and teeth"],"e":"His illustration: with total body water on one side and adipose tissue on the other, a highly lipid-soluble drug would mostly (he said 90%, as an example) go into adipose tissue. Diazepam is the slide example.","w":["That suits small water-soluble drugs.","It goes mainly to fat.","Plasma holds large or charged drugs.","That is for certain ions."],"k":"Lipid-soluble → fat.","rn":[["","Which molecules distribute into adipose tissue?","Which molecules distribute into adipose tissue?","Highly lipid-soluble molecules"],["","Diazepam","Diazepam","Adipose tissue"]]},{"id":29067,"t":"Distribution","s":"Fluoride from toothpaste accumulates in which tissue compartment?","e":"Fluoride accumulates in bone and teeth.","k":"Bone and teeth.","a":["bone and teeth","bone","teeth","bones and teeth","bone/teeth"],"rn":[["","Fluoride","Fluoride","Bone and teeth"]]},{"id":29068,"t":"Distribution","s":"Ethanol is a small water-soluble molecule. In which compartment does it distribute?","e":"Ethanol distributes throughout total body water.","k":"Total body water.","a":["total body water","body water","tbw","all body water"],"rn":[["","Ethanol","Ethanol","Total body water"],["","Which molecules distribute into total body water?","Which molecules distribute into total body water?","Small, water-soluble molecules"]]},{"id":29069,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"A student says “pharmacodynamics is how the liver handles a drug.” What is the best correction?","o":["That is PK; PD is the mechanism of action","That is toxicology; pharmacodynamics is distribution into tissues","That is correct, as metabolism is pharmacodynamics","That is therapeutics; pharmacodynamics is prescribing","That is pharmacogenomics"],"e":"Pharmacokinetics is what the body does to the drug, including metabolism in the liver (ADME). Pharmacodynamics is what the drug does to the body – basically its mechanism of action. Memory aid: dynaMics = what the drug does to Me.","w":["Liver handling is PK; PD is mechanism of action.","Metabolism is PK.","Therapeutics is treatment.","That is genetic variation."],"k":"Liver handling = PK.","rn":[["","Pharmacodynamics","Pharmacodynamics","What the drug does to the body; Mechanism of action of drugs"],["","What 3 things does pharmacology study about drugs?","What 3 things does pharmacology study about drugs?","Structure, targets, mechanisms of action; Distribution in and handling by the body; Effects on the body"]]},{"id":29070,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which pair matches the right kind of question to each branch?","o":["PD: how does salbutamol act? PK: how is it eliminated?","PD: how is salbutamol eliminated? PK: how does it act?","PD: what is its brand? PK: what is its class?","PD: what dose? PK: which BNF section?","Both: what is its chemical name?"],"e":"PD covers mechanism of action (what the drug does to the body); PK covers ADME (what the body does to the drug), including elimination.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PD = action; PK = handling."},{"id":29071,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"A patient who is vomiting cannot keep tablets down. Which route consideration applies?","o":["It is a symptom, but correct that oral cannot be used","It is a clinical condition requiring IV in all cases","Vomiting increases first-pass metabolism","Vomiting is a reason to use the oral route","It was not discussed"],"e":"A student suggested vomiting; he said that is a symptom rather than a clinical condition, but correct in that the patient cannot take medicine orally. Another student said angina (sublingual, rapid treatment).","w":["He did not say IV in all cases.","Not stated.","Not.","It was discussed."],"k":"Vomiting: symptom; can’t use oral.","rn":[["","When could rectal or non-oral routes be needed?","When could rectal or non-oral routes be needed?","Patient unconscious; Patient vomiting (cannot take orally)"],["","Why can you not give oral drugs to an unconscious patient?","Why can you not give oral drugs to an unconscious patient?","They cannot take drugs orally"]]},{"id":29072,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"Why does sublingual nitrate act faster than the same drug swallowed?","o":["It enters the systemic circulation and avoids first pass","It is metabolised more quickly by the liver","It is absorbed in the stomach rather than the intestine","It binds more strongly to albumin","It is excreted more slowly by the kidney"],"e":"The absorption figure notes drugs taken sublingually avoid first-pass metabolism. Under the tongue the drug is absorbed quickly into the systemic circulation, which is why sublingual suits angina.","w":["It avoids the liver first.","It is absorbed under the tongue.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sublingual avoids first pass.","rn":[["","Which route avoids first-pass metabolism and is placed under the tongue?","Which route avoids first-pass metabolism and is placed under the tongue?","Sublingual"],["","First-pass Metabolism","First-pass Metabolism","Oral drugs pass through the liver before the systemic circulation"]]},{"id":29073,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","f":"ph23","s":"How does an orally absorbed drug reach the liver?","o":["Via mesenteric capillaries and the hepatic portal vein","Via the hepatic artery directly from the stomach","Via the lymph to the thoracic duct","Via the oesophagus","Via the rectum"],"e":"Drug absorbed into the mesenteric capillary network is carried to the liver via the hepatic portal vein and may be metabolised before reaching the systemic circulation. Labels also note acid-labile drugs, enteric-coated tablets, mucosal and bacterial metabolism, and suppositories at the rectum.","w":["The figure shows the portal vein.","Not shown.","The oesophagus carries the swallowed drug, not absorbed drug.","Suppositories are given rectally."],"k":"Gut → portal vein → liver.","rn":[["","How do drugs absorbed from the intestine reach the liver?","How do drugs absorbed from the intestine reach the liver?","Mesenteric capillary network; Hepatic portal vein"]]},{"id":29074,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","f":"ph23","s":"Which threats does an oral drug face before absorption?","o":["Gastric acid, mucosal enzymes, gut bacteria","Albumin binding in the gut wall and renal excretion before absorption","Biliary excretion and sweat","Adipose storage and bone uptake","Cytochrome P450 in the lungs only"],"e":"Labels note acid-labile drugs destroyed by gastric acid, enteric-coated tablets, metabolism by enzymes in mucosal cells and by intestinal bacteria, before the drug reaches the portal vein and liver.","w":["Those come after absorption.","Excretion routes.","Distribution compartments.","Not labelled."],"k":"Gut threats: acid, mucosal enzymes, bacteria.","rn":[["","What else can metabolise drugs in the gut?","What else can metabolise drugs in the gut?","Intestinal bacteria"],["","What happens to acid-labile drugs in the stomach?","What happens to acid-labile drugs in the stomach?","Destroyed by gastric acid"],["","How can drugs be lost in the intestine wall?","How can drugs be lost in the intestine wall?","Metabolism by enzymes in mucosal cells; Efflux by transporter proteins"]]},{"id":29075,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"Which of the following best explains why oral drugs take time to act?","o":["Absorption and liver passage take about an hour","They must first bind to albumin in the stomach","They must be excreted before they act","They are converted into brand names","They distribute only into bone"],"e":"Orally, the lecturer said it might take about one hour to reach the systemic circulation, because the drug is absorbed from the gut and passes the liver. IV goes directly into the systemic circulation.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Oral ≈ 1 hour.","rn":[["","How long might an oral drug take to reach the systemic circulation?","How long might an oral drug take to reach the systemic circulation?","About 1 hour"]]},{"id":29076,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A drug’s metabolite is responsible for liver damage. Which metabolite outcome category does this fall into?","o":["Active (toxic effect)","Inactive","Less active","Activated (pro-drug) only","Excreted unchanged"],"e":"Metabolites can be inactive, less active, active (therapeutic or toxic effect) or activated (pro-drug). A metabolite causing damage is active with a toxic effect.","w":["Inactive has no effect.","Less active.","Pro-drug activation forms the intended active drug.","Not a metabolite outcome."],"k":"Toxic metabolite = active.","rn":[["","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","Inactive; Less active; Active (therapeutic / toxic effect); Activated (pro-drug)"]]},{"id":29077,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Which pairing of ADME phase and organ is most correct?","o":["Metabolism – liver; excretion – kidney","Metabolism – kidney; excretion – liver","Absorption – kidney; excretion – gut","Distribution – liver only","Excretion – adipose tissue"],"e":"The major organ of metabolism is the liver (70–80%, cytochrome P450). The main excretion route is renal (kidney).","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Distribution is to tissues.","Not."],"k":"Liver metabolises; kidney excretes.","rn":[["","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","Liver; Kidney; GI tract; Lungs; Skin"]]},{"id":29078,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"A drug causes a desirable response at low dose but harm at high dose. Which terms describe these?","o":["Efficacy and toxicity","Absorption and excretion","Induction and inhibition","Generic and brand","Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics"],"e":"Pharmacology covers effects on the body, including desirable responses (efficacy) and undesirable side effects (toxicity). If the dose becomes higher you see adverse effects, i.e. toxicity.","w":["PK phases.","Enzyme effects.","Names.","Branches."],"k":"Efficacy vs toxicity.","rn":[["","Desirable responses to a drug are called what?","Desirable responses to a drug are called what?","Efficacy"],["","Undesirable side-effects of a drug are called what?","Undesirable side-effects of a drug are called what?","Toxicity"]]},{"id":29079,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Which situation is an example of medication error rather than an adverse drug reaction?","o":["Wrong dose or wrong time","A rash caused by a correctly prescribed drug at the right dose","Expected side effects at a correct dose","A drug metabolised normally","A pro-drug activated in the liver"],"e":"Medication error in the table includes drug-drug interaction, wrong drug, wrong dose, inadequate monitoring, contraindication and wrong time (about 46% of ADEs). Adverse drug reactions were about 39%.","w":["That is an adverse drug reaction.","That is an ADR.","Not an event.","Normal."],"k":"Medication error examples.","rn":[["","What types of problem count as medication errors?","What types of problem count as medication errors?","Wrong dose; Wrong frequency; Wrong drug; Wrong time"],["","Which medication errors were the rarest cause of adverse drug events?","Which medication errors were the rarest cause of adverse drug events?","Contraindication (2); Wrong time (2)"]]},{"id":29080,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Medication errors in the study could be related to whom?","o":["Patient, consultant or pharmacist – or several","Only the pharmacist who dispensed the drug to the patient","Only the patient","Only the drug company","Only the consultant"],"e":"About 46% of ADEs were medication errors – issues with dose, frequency and so on – which could be patient-, consultant- or pharmacist-related, or several together.","w":["Errors could be patient-, consultant- or pharmacist-related.","Not only.","Not mentioned.","Not only."],"k":"Errors: patient/consultant/pharmacist.","rn":[["","Who can be involved in a medication error?","Who can be involved in a medication error?","Patient; Consultant; Pharmacist; Multiple people"]]},{"id":29081,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Which of the following is listed as part of the “size of the problem”?","o":["More than 1 billion prescription items per year","More than 1 million prescription items per year","About 100 million prescription items per year","Prescription errors are no longer seen","Over-the-counter drugs are excluded"],"e":"The slide lists >1 billion prescription items dispensed per year; over-the-counter, food, vitamins; prescription errors; adverse effects and hospital admissions due to adverse effects. Solution: improving prescribing by continuum education.","w":["It is over a billion.","Not stated.","Errors are listed.","OTC is listed."],"k":">1 billion items/year.","rn":[["","What are 4 reasons for studying pharmacology and therapeutics?","What are 4 reasons for studying pharmacology and therapeutics?",">1 billion prescription items/year; Over-the-counter, food, vitamins; Prescription errors; Adverse effects and hospital admissions"],["","About how many prescription items are dispensed per year?","About how many prescription items are dispensed per year?",">1 billion"]]},{"id":29082,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Which statement about why pharmacology matters is given as a key point?","o":["It is essential to optimise therapeutics and prescribing","It replaces the need for physiology when choosing a drug for a patient","It is only useful for pharmacists","It matters only in Year 5","It is needed only for herbal remedies"],"e":"Key take-home: studying the pharmacology of the drug is essential for optimising therapeutics and prescribing. Understanding pharmacology is essential to MDs, researchers and all healthcare professionals.","w":["Buchheim said therapeutics must combine with physiology.","For all healthcare professionals.","All five years.","Not."],"k":"Pharmacology → optimise prescribing.","rn":[["","What is the key take-home message about the pharmacology of a drug?","What is the key take-home message about the pharmacology of a drug?","Studying it is essential for optimising therapeutics and prescribing"]]},{"id":29083,"t":"Development of Pharmacology","s":"Which statement about the 19th-century view of Rudolph Buchheim is correct?","o":["Therapeutics must combine with physiology","Like cures like, with dilution enhancing the remedy’s activity","Bloodletting and purgatives should be used","Drugs act by vital forces","Worms and moss are effective medicines"],"e":"Buchheim: therapeutics was in an empirical stage; it is by combination with physiology that it must rise to be a science. The lecturer: “if you know the physiology you can know the mechanism of the drugs”.","w":["That is homeopathy.","Allopathy.","Ehrlich rejected vital forces.","Boyle."],"k":"Buchheim: combine with physiology.","rn":[["","Rudolph Buchheim (19th century) said therapeutics must combine with what to become a sc…","Rudolph Buchheim (19th century) said therapeutics must combine with what to become a science?","Physiology"]]},{"id":29085,"t":"What is a Drug","f":"ph15","s":"A drug is described as “acts on β2 receptors”. On which basis is it classified?","o":["Mode or site of action","Chemical structure","Therapeutic use","BNF body system","Route of administration"],"e":"β2-agonists are the slide example of classification by mode or site of action: drugs binding a specific receptor, e.g. the beta-2 receptor (on smooth muscle and other tissues).","w":["Benzodiazepines.","Anticonvulsant.","BNF uses system numbers.","Not a basis."],"k":"β2-agonist = mode of action.","rn":[["","β2-agonists are an example of which classification?","β2-agonists are an example of which classification?","Mode or site of action"]]},{"id":29086,"t":"Excretion","s":"Where is the β2 receptor described as present?","o":["Smooth muscle and various other parts of the body","Only in the liver, where β2-agonists are metabolised by P450","Only in the kidney","Only in bone","Only in plasma"],"e":"By mode of action: drugs binding a specific receptor, e.g. the beta-2 receptor, present on smooth muscle and various other parts of the body.","w":["He said smooth muscle and other parts of the body.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"β2: smooth muscle and elsewhere.","rn":[["","Which 2 other body parts can some drugs be excreted in?","Which 2 other body parts can some drugs be excreted in?","Hair; Nails"],["","Distribution","Distribution","Transfer of a compound from the general circulation to other parts of the body and into the tissues"]]},{"id":29087,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"Which disciplines are listed alongside pharmacodynamics and pharmacokinetics?","o":["Pharmacy, toxicology, pharmacogenomics, medicinal chemistry","Anatomy, histology, physiology, embryology and cell biology","Surgery, radiology, pathology, psychiatry","Microbiology, virology, immunology, genetics","Biochemistry, statistics, ethics, law"],"e":"Disciplines: pharmacodynamics, pharmacokinetics, pharmacy, toxicology, pharmacogenetics/genomics, medicinal chemistry, clinical pharmacology and molecular pharmacology.","w":["Those are not pharmacology disciplines.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Pharmacology disciplines.","rn":[["","What are the 8 subdivisions of pharmacology?","What are the 8 subdivisions of pharmacology?","Pharmacodynamics; Pharmacokinetics; Pharmacy; Toxicology; Pharmacogenetics / genomics; Medicinal Chemistry; Clinical pharmacology; Molecular pharmacology"]]},{"id":29088,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"A patient needs a drug acting on the skin surface. Which administration category applies?","o":["Other epithelial surfaces","Injection","Sublingual or buccal","Inhalation","Rectal"],"e":"Other epithelial surfaces: skin, cornea, vagina and nasal mucosa. Injection includes IV, IM and SC.","w":["IV/IM/SC.","Mouth.","Lungs.","Rectum."],"k":"Skin = other epithelial surface.","rn":[["","Which other epithelial surfaces can drugs be given through?","Which other epithelial surfaces can drugs be given through?","Skin; Cornea; Vagina; Nasal mucosa"]]},{"id":29089,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"IM and SC are examples of which route?","o":["Injection","Oral","Inhalation","Sublingual","Other epithelial surfaces"],"e":"Injection includes IV (intravenous), IM (intramuscular) and SC (subcutaneous).","w":["p.o.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"IM/SC/IV = injection.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of injection?","What are the 3 types of injection?","IV (intravenous); IM (intramuscular); SC (subcutaneous)"]]},{"id":29090,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"What does “p.o.” mean on a prescription?","e":"p.o. = per os (by mouth), the most common route.","k":"By mouth.","a":["by mouth","orally","oral","per os","by the mouth"],"rn":[["","p.o.","p.o.","per os (by mouth)"]]},{"id":29092,"t":"Excretion","s":"The pharmacokinetics summary (Rang & Dale ch. 9) lists which routes of elimination?","o":["Urine, faeces, milk/sweat, expired air","Urine only, after renal filtration","Bile and sweat only","Bone and teeth","Adipose tissue and plasma"],"e":"The summary figure has three columns: administration, absorption and distribution (to plasma, brain, placenta/fetus, breast and sweat glands; portal system → liver → bile), and elimination: urine, faeces, milk/sweat and expired air.","w":["The figure lists urine, faeces, milk/sweat and expired air.","Not only.","Compartments.","Compartments."],"k":"Elimination routes.","rn":[["","Which 5 routes eliminate drugs?","Which 5 routes eliminate drugs?","Urine; Faeces (bile); Milk; Sweat; Expired air"]]},{"id":29093,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which list contains only routes of drug administration?","o":["Oral/rectal, percutaneous, IV, IM, intrathecal, inhalation","Oral, sublingual and buccal only, as all others are injection routes","IV and IM only","Inhalation and buccal only","Rectal and nasal only"],"e":"Administration column: oral or rectal, percutaneous, intravenous, intramuscular, intrathecal and inhalation.","w":["The figure lists six routes.","Not only.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PK summary routes.","rn":[["","What are the 6 routes of administration?","What are the 6 routes of administration?","Oral; Injection; Inhalation; Sublingual or buccal; Rectal; Other epithelial surfaces"]]},{"id":29096,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"Which statement about William W. Chin’s quotation is correct?","o":["Personalised medicine: right drug, patient, dose, time","It says poisons in small doses are the best medicines for most patients","It says drugs act by vital forces","It says homeopathy is effective","It defines pharmacokinetics"],"e":"Chin (in Golan et al.): the human need for medicines to alleviate suffering, cure disease and delay aging; the goal of personalised medicine is the right drug for the right patient at the right dose and time, improving drug safety.","w":["That is Withering.","Ehrlich rejected that.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Chin: personalised medicine.","rn":[["","What does personalised medicine aim to identify?","What does personalised medicine aim to identify?","Right drug; Right patient; Right dose and time"],["","Personalised Medicine","Personalised Medicine","The right drug for the right patient"]]},{"id":29098,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Why are drug interactions relevant to pharmacokinetics?","o":["Patients may take several drugs that interact","Interactions only occur with herbal remedies, never with prescribed drugs","Interactions only affect excretion","Interactions are pharmacodynamic only","Interactions only happen IV"],"e":"Patients sometimes take one drug, sometimes several, so interactions can happen: you need to know whether two or three drugs together are right. Interactions are listed among the reasons PK is relevant.","w":["Any combination can interact.","Metabolism too.","PK too.","Not."],"k":"PK: interactions.","rn":[["","Why does knowing pharmacokinetics help when giving several drugs?","Why does knowing pharmacokinetics help when giving several drugs?","Drugs can interact; Check that the drugs are safe together"]]},{"id":29100,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"Which statement summarises why route of administration is part of pharmacokinetics’ relevance?","o":["Route sets access, e.g. no oral if unconscious","Route determines the drug’s chemical name and BNF section","Route determines the BNF section","Route determines the drug class","Route is irrelevant"],"e":"Route of administration is listed in PK’s relevance: “if the patient is unconscious you cannot give drugs orally”. Route influences absorption and first-pass metabolism.","w":["Route affects absorption and access.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Route affects access.","rn":[["","Why can you not give oral drugs to an unconscious patient?","Why can you not give oral drugs to an unconscious patient?","They cannot take drugs orally"],["","What is the most common route of administration?","What is the most common route of administration?","Oral"]]},{"id":29101,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Which effect would enzymatic inhibition have on first-pass metabolism of an oral drug?","o":["Decrease it","Increase it","No effect","Abolish absorption","Convert it to IV"],"e":"Inhibition: therapeutic effect ↑, toxicity ↑, first-pass metabolism ↓, metabolism rate ↓. Induction is the opposite.","w":["That is induction.","It changes.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inhibition ↓ first pass."},{"id":29102,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Memory aid: “INhibitor = drug stays IN.” What does this mean?","o":["Slower metabolism keeps plasma levels up","Faster metabolism keeps plasma levels up","The drug stays in adipose tissue","The drug stays in the gut","The drug stays in the kidney"],"e":"An inhibitor slows metabolism, so the drug stays in (level up, risk of toxicity). An inducer makes the drug disappear (level down, sub-therapeutic).","w":["Faster lowers levels.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inhibitor: level up.","rn":[["","Why does an enzyme inhibitor raise drug A levels?","Why does an enzyme inhibitor raise drug A levels?","Less metabolism of drug A"]]},{"id":29103,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Which statement about metabolising sites is correct?","o":["Liver mainly; also kidney, gut, lungs, skin","Kidney mainly; the liver only handles pro-drugs","Lungs only","Skin mainly","Plasma only"],"e":"Sites of metabolism: LIVER (highlighted), kidney, GI tract, lungs and skin. The liver does 70–80%, with minimal metabolism elsewhere.","w":["The liver is the major site.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Metabolism sites.","rn":[["","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","What are the 5 sites of drug metabolism?","Liver; Kidney; GI tract; Lungs; Skin"]]},{"id":29104,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"A drug is classified under BNF chapter 3. What does this tell you?","o":["It acts on the respiratory system","It is a benzodiazepine acting on the brain","It is an anticonvulsant","It is a pro-drug","It is taken by mouth"],"e":"The BNF classifies by body system; chapter 3 is the respiratory system (e.g. 3.1 bronchodilators). You will meet the BNF in Year 3.","w":["BNF chapter 3 is the respiratory system.","Therapeutic use.","Not.","Not."],"k":"BNF 3 = respiratory.","rn":[["","In the BNF, what are 3, 3.1, 3.1.1 and 3.1.1.1?","In the BNF, what are 3, 3.1, 3.1.1 and 3.1.1.1?","3: Respiratory system; 3.1: Bronchodilators; 3.1.1: Adrenoceptor agonists; 3.1.1.1: Selective beta2 agonists"]]},{"id":29108,"t":"Development of Pharmacology","s":"Which comparison of herbal remedies, homeopathy and conventional medicines is correct?","o":["Homeopathy: like cures like; conventional: drug–tissue chemistry","Homeopathy rests on drug–tissue chemistry; conventional medicine on like cures like","All three are scientific to the same degree","Herbal remedies are the newest approach","Conventional medicines rely on vital forces"],"e":"Homeopathy (19th century): like cures like, activity enhanced by dilution. Ehrlich (20th century) set conventional understanding as chemical interactions between drugs and tissues. Herbal remedies and magical potions came earliest.","w":["Swapped.","Scientific use is recent.","They are the oldest.","Ehrlich rejected vital forces."],"k":"Homeopathy vs conventional.","rn":[["","Homeopathy","Homeopathy","19th century; Like cures like; Activity enhanced by dilution"]]},{"id":29109,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"Which best explains why an IV drug acts within minutes but the same oral drug in about an hour?","o":["IV goes straight into the circulation","IV is metabolised by gut enzymes first, which speeds its action","IV is stored in adipose first","IV is excreted more slowly","IV binds more albumin"],"e":"IV enters the systemic circulation directly, avoiding the liver. Oral must be absorbed and pass the liver (first pass), which may take about an hour.","w":["Gut enzymes act on oral drugs.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"IV direct → fast.","rn":[["","Why is IV better than oral in an emergency?","Why is IV better than oral in an emergency?","Skips the liver; Goes directly into systemic circulation; Very quick effect"]]},{"id":29110,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Which is the best description of “dose adjustment” as a reason PK matters?","o":["Changing dose when drug handling changes","Changing the brand when the dose needs changing","Changing the class when a drug fails","Changing the chemical name","Never changing doses"],"e":"Dose adjustment is among the PK relevance points: e.g. with an inducer the effective dose falls (100 mg acting like 50 mg), and interactions or individual factors change plasma levels.","w":["Dose adjustment changes the dose.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dose adjustment.","rn":[["","Where is a pro-drug changed into its active form?","Where is a pro-drug changed into its active form?","The liver"]]},{"id":29111,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"Which of these is a substance studied by pharmacology but NOT a medicine?","o":["Caffeine","Salbutamol","Amoxicillin","Ramipril","Heparin"],"e":"Pharmacology also covers caffeine, nicotine, alcohol, abused substances (cannabis, heroin), food constituents (vitamins, minerals, amino acids) and cosmetics.","w":["Medicine.","Medicine.","Medicine.","Medicine."],"k":"Caffeine counts as a drug.","rn":[["","What 4 groups of substances count as drugs besides medicines?","What 4 groups of substances count as drugs besides medicines?","Caffeine, nicotine, alcohol; Abused chemicals (cannabis, heroin); Food constituents (vitamins, minerals, amino acids); Cosmetics"]]},{"id":29112,"t":"Distribution","s":"Mannitol is a larger water-soluble molecule than ethanol. Why does it stay in extracellular water?","o":["Its size keeps it out of cells","It is highly lipid-soluble","It is highly charged and protein-bound","It is an ion stored in bone","It is destroyed by gastric acid"],"e":"Total body water holds small water-soluble molecules (ethanol); extracellular water holds larger water-soluble molecules (mannitol). Size limits where it can go.","w":["That is diazepam.","That is heparin.","That is fluoride.","Not."],"k":"Mannitol: size → extracellular."},{"id":29113,"t":"Distribution","s":"Which statement about drug distribution is correct?","o":["It depends on the drug; most bind albumin","All drugs distribute equally to all tissues once absorbed","No drugs bind plasma proteins","Distribution happens before absorption","Distribution is mainly renal"],"e":"Distribution depends on the drug’s characteristics (water, plasma, fat, bone); most drugs bind proteins, especially albumin.","w":["It depends on the drug.","Most do.","After absorption.","Excretion is renal."],"k":"Distribution depends on drug.","rn":[["","What do most drugs bind to?","What do most drugs bind to?","Proteins; Especially albumin"],["","What does distribution of a drug depend on?","What does distribution of a drug depend on?","Characteristics of the drug itself"]]},{"id":29115,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A drug is cleared mainly by the liver. Which phase would a liver disease most directly affect?","o":["Metabolism","Absorption from the skin","Distribution into bone","Inhalation","Brand naming"],"e":"The liver is the major organ of drug metabolism (70–80%, cytochrome P450). Medical conditions are listed among factors affecting induction/inhibition.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not a phase.","Not."],"k":"Liver → metabolism.","rn":[["","About what percentage of drug metabolism happens in the liver?","About what percentage of drug metabolism happens in the liver?","70 to 80%"]]},{"id":29116,"t":"Excretion","s":"Why is renal function relevant to most drugs?","o":["The kidney is the main route of excretion","The kidney is the main site of metabolism","The kidney is the main site of absorption","The kidney stores most drugs","The kidney produces albumin"],"e":"Excretion is mainly renal; others are biliary, respiratory, dermal and faecal. The liver, not the kidney, is the main site of metabolism.","w":["Liver.","Not.","Albumin/adipose.","Not."],"k":"Kidney: main excretion.","rn":[["","What is the main route of drug excretion?","What is the main route of drug excretion?","Renal"]]},{"id":29118,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"A patient with a severe infection cannot take oral drugs and needs rapid effect. Which route is most appropriate?","o":["IV","Oral","Rectal","Inhalation","Topical skin"],"e":"IV is used when absolutely necessary – e.g. a serious infection or emergency – because it goes straight into the systemic circulation, avoiding the liver.","w":["Cannot take oral.","For unconscious patients; slower.","For asthma.","Not."],"k":"Severe/emergency → IV.","rn":[["","Why is IV better than oral in an emergency?","Why is IV better than oral in an emergency?","Skips the liver; Goes directly into systemic circulation; Very quick effect"]]},{"id":29120,"t":"Metabolism","s":"Drug A is given with drug B (an inhibitor) and separately with drug C (an inducer). Which ranking of drug A plasma levels is expected?","o":["A+B > A alone > A+C","A+C > A alone > A+B","A alone > A+B > A+C","A+B = A+C > A alone","All equal"],"e":"Inhibitor B slows metabolism, so A+B rises towards toxicity; A alone stays between the lines; inducer C speeds metabolism, so A+C falls towards sub-therapeutic.","w":["Reversed.","A+B is highest.","They differ.","They differ."],"k":"Inhibitor > alone > inducer."},{"id":29121,"t":"Absorption and First Pass Metabolism","s":"A 60-year-old man is given a drug by mouth and only a small fraction reaches his general circulation. When the same dose is given intravenously, blood levels are much higher. His pharmacist explains that the oral drug passes through one organ before reaching the rest of the body.<br><br>What explains the difference, and how can it be avoided?","o":["First-pass metabolism in the liver via the hepatic portal system; give IV or sublingually","Renal excretion of the oral drug; give it rectally","Binding of the IV drug to albumin; give it orally","Distribution into fat; give it by inhalation","Enzyme induction in the lungs; give a pro-drug orally so it is activated in the lungs"],"e":"Step 1: after oral administration, the drug is absorbed from the gut into the hepatic portal system and passes through the liver before the systemic circulation, where much is metabolised (first-pass metabolism; on the slide 100% swallowed → 20% to the rest of the body). Step 2: IV administration goes directly into the systemic circulation, avoiding first pass, as does the sublingual route.","w":["Excretion is not the reason for low oral bioavailability here.","Albumin binding does not explain why oral levels are lower.","Distribution into fat does not explain the oral–IV difference.","First pass is in the liver, and pro-drugs need activation, not avoidance."],"k":"Oral → liver first pass (100% → 20%); IV/sublingual avoid it.","rn":[["","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","What is the route of a swallowed drug in first-pass metabolism?","Digestive system (enzymes); Hepatic portal system; Liver (enzymes); Rest of the body"]]},{"id":29122,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A 70-year-old woman takes a medicine that is metabolised by a cytochrome P450 enzyme. She starts a second drug that inhibits this enzyme. A week later, she develops side effects typical of too much of the first medicine.<br><br>What has happened to her first drug's metabolism and plasma level?","o":["Metabolism slowed, plasma level rose, so toxicity increased","Metabolism sped up, plasma level fell, so treatment failed","Metabolism slowed, plasma level fell","Metabolism unchanged; the second drug blocked excretion in the hair","Metabolism sped up, plasma level rose, as induction increases first-pass metabolism"],"e":"Step 1: enzymatic inhibition means slower metabolism, so less drug is broken down. Step 2: slower metabolism raises plasma levels, increasing the therapeutic effect and the risk of toxicity, which fits her side effects.","w":["That describes enzyme induction, not inhibition.","Slower metabolism raises, not lowers, the plasma level.","Inhibition affects metabolism.","Induction speeds metabolism and lowers plasma levels."],"k":"Inhibitor → slower metabolism → ↑ level → toxicity.","rn":[["","Why does an enzyme inhibitor raise drug A levels?","Why does an enzyme inhibitor raise drug A levels?","Less metabolism of drug A"],["","Enzyme inhibition: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Enzyme inhibition: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Therapeutic effect: up; Toxicity: up; First pass metabolism: down; Metabolism rate: down"],["","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Enzyme induction: effect on therapeutic effect, toxicity, first pass and metabolism rate","Therapeutic effect: down; Toxicity: down; First pass metabolism: up; Metabolism rate: up"]]},{"id":29123,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A 40-year-old man takes drug A, which is active as swallowed, and drug B, a pro-drug that must be metabolised to become active. He then starts a medicine that induces the liver enzymes that metabolise both drugs.<br><br>What is the likely effect on each drug?","o":["A: less effective (faster breakdown); B: more active drug formed faster","A: more toxic; B: less active","Both become more toxic","Neither changes, as induction only affects excretion","A: more effective; B: unchanged, as pro-drugs are never metabolised by liver enzymes"],"e":"Step 1: enzymatic induction means faster metabolism, lowering plasma levels of an active drug, with a risk of a sub-therapeutic effect. Step 2: a pro-drug is an inactive precursor that must be metabolised into the active drug, so faster metabolism converts it into the active form more quickly.","w":["Induction lowers drug A's level rather than causing toxicity.","Faster breakdown reduces A's effect.","Induction affects metabolism.","Pro-drugs must be metabolised to become active."],"k":"Inducer: ↓ active drug levels; ↑ activation of pro-drugs.","rn":[["","Where is a pro-drug changed into its active form?","Where is a pro-drug changed into its active form?","The liver"]]},{"id":29124,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"A pharmacology student writes two reports on a new asthma drug. Report 1 describes how the drug binds β2-receptors in the airways and relaxes the smooth muscle. Report 2 describes how it is absorbed through the lungs, spread around the body, broken down by the liver and removed by the kidneys.<br><br>Which branches of pharmacology do the reports cover?","o":["1: pharmacodynamics; 2: pharmacokinetics (ADME)","1: pharmacokinetics; 2: pharmacodynamics","Both pharmacodynamics","1: toxicology; 2: medicinal chemistry","Both pharmacokinetics, because both describe what happens to the drug in the body"],"e":"Step 1: pharmacodynamics is \"what the drug does to the body\", i.e. its mechanism of action, such as binding β2-receptors. Step 2: pharmacokinetics is \"what the body does to the drug\": absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion (ADME).","w":["The labels are swapped.","Report 2 describes ADME, which is pharmacokinetics.","These are different disciplines.","Report 1 describes mechanism, which is pharmacodynamics."],"k":"Pharmacodynamics = drug → body; pharmacokinetics = body → drug (ADME).","rn":[["","What are the 2 main branches of pharmacology?","What are the 2 main branches of pharmacology?","Pharmacodynamics; Pharmacokinetics"],["","What are the 4 phases of pharmacokinetics?","What are the 4 phases of pharmacokinetics?","Absorption; Distribution; Metabolism; Excretion; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":29125,"t":"Distribution","s":"A doctor compares where four drugs end up after IV injection. Drug W is small and water-soluble and spreads through total body water. Drug X is highly charged and very large and stays in the blood plasma. Drug Y is highly lipid-soluble and builds up in fat. Drug Z is an ion that collects in bone and teeth.<br><br>Which examples from the lecture match W, X, Y and Z?","o":["W: ethanol; X: heparin; Y: diazepam; Z: fluoride","W: heparin; X: ethanol; Y: fluoride; Z: diazepam","W: mannitol; X: diazepam; Y: heparin; Z: ethanol","W: diazepam; X: fluoride; Y: ethanol; Z: heparin","W: ethanol; X: mannitol; Y: heparin; Z: diazepam, as lipid-soluble drugs stay in plasma"],"e":"Step 1: small water-soluble molecules (ethanol) distribute in total body water, larger water-soluble ones (mannitol) in extracellular water, and highly protein-bound, very large or highly charged molecules (heparin) stay in plasma. Step 2: highly lipid-soluble molecules (diazepam) go to adipose tissue, and certain ions (fluoride) go to bone and teeth.","w":["Heparin stays in plasma; ethanol spreads through body water.","Mannitol is extracellular, and diazepam goes to fat.","Diazepam goes to fat, and fluoride to bone.","Diazepam (lipid-soluble) goes to fat, not plasma."],"k":"Ethanol: body water; heparin: plasma; diazepam: fat; fluoride: bone."},{"id":29126,"t":"Pharmacokinetics and Administration","s":"A 75-year-old man with chest pain cannot swallow and needs a drug that avoids first-pass metabolism quickly. A 3-year-old who is vomiting needs a medicine that cannot be given by mouth. A 30-year-old with asthma needs a drug delivered straight to the airways.<br><br>Which routes best suit these three patients?","o":["Sublingual; rectal (suppository); inhalation","Oral; oral; IV","Rectal; sublingual; oral","IM; oral; skin","Inhalation; IV; per os, as oral is the most common and needs no special skills"],"e":"Step 1: the sublingual route avoids first-pass metabolism and needs no swallowing, while the rectal route (suppository) is an option when the oral route cannot be used, as in a vomiting child. Step 2: inhalation is used for asthma drugs such as salbutamol to reach the airways.","w":["Oral routes are unsuitable for a patient who cannot swallow or is vomiting.","Sublingual suits the first patient; inhalation suits asthma.","IM and skin do not match these needs best.","Oral is not suitable for the vomiting child."],"k":"Sublingual avoids first pass; rectal when oral impossible; inhalation for asthma.","rn":[["","What are the 6 routes of administration?","What are the 6 routes of administration?","Oral; Injection; Inhalation; Sublingual or buccal; Rectal; Other epithelial surfaces"]]},{"id":29127,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"A junior doctor writes \"Ventolin inhaler\" on a drug chart. The pharmacist asks her to change it, explaining that the same drug is sold under several brand names and also has a long chemical name, but there is one name doctors should always use.<br><br>Which name should be used, and what is it for this drug?","o":["The generic name: salbutamol","The brand name: Ventolin","The chemical name: (RS)-4-[2-(tert-butylamino)-1-hydroxyethyl]-2-(hydroxymethyl)phenol","The class name only: selective β2-agonist","Any brand name, such as Airomir, Asmasal, Salamol or Salbulin, as long as it is spelt correctly"],"e":"Step 1: drugs have a chemical name, a generic name and brand names; for salbutamol, the brands include Airomir, Asmasal, Salamol, Salbulin and Ventolin. Step 2: the lecture stresses: always use generic names, so the chart should say salbutamol.","w":["Brand names should not be used.","The chemical name is not used for prescribing.","The class name does not identify the specific drug.","Brand names should not be used."],"k":"Always use generic names (salbutamol, not Ventolin).","rn":[["","Salbutamol: types of drug name","Salbutamol: types of drug name","Chemical: long systematic name; Generic: salbutamol; Brand: Ventolin"],["","Which drug names should you ALWAYS use?","Which drug names should you ALWAYS use?","Generic names"],["","Name 5 brand names of salbutamol.","Name 5 brand names of salbutamol.","Airomir; Asmasal; Salamol; Salbulin; Ventolin"]]},{"id":29128,"t":"What is a Drug","s":"A student notes three ways a drug might be grouped. Salbutamol is grouped by where and how it acts on receptors. Diazepam belongs to a group defined by its shared chemical structure. A third drug is grouped because it is used to prevent seizures.<br><br>Which bases of classification are these?","o":["Mode/site of action (β2-agonists); chemical structure (benzodiazepines); therapeutic use (anticonvulsant)","Therapeutic use; mode of action; chemical structure","Chemical structure; therapeutic use; mode of action","Brand name; generic name; chemical name","All three are BNF chapters on the respiratory system, which is the only uniform system"],"e":"Step 1: there is no uniform system of classification; drugs can be classified by mode or site of action (e.g. β2-agonists) or by chemical structure (e.g. benzodiazepines). Step 2: they can also be classified by therapeutic use (e.g. anticonvulsant); the BNF classification is widely used.","w":["The bases are in the wrong order.","Salbutamol is grouped by mode of action.","These are naming types, not classifications.","There is no uniform system, and only salbutamol is a respiratory drug."],"k":"Classify by action, structure or therapeutic use; no uniform system.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","What are the 3 ways to classify drugs?","Mode or site of action; Chemical structure; Therapeutic use"]]},{"id":29129,"t":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics at KMMS","s":"A hospital audit finds three prescribing errors. In error 1, a drug intended for one patient was given to the patient in the next bed. In error 2, a drug was given IV when it should have been oral. In error 3, a drug was given in the morning instead of the evening as prescribed.<br><br>Which of the \"Six Rights\" of rational prescribing were breached?","o":["Right patient, right route and right time","Right drug, right dose and right outcome","Right patient, right dose and right drug","Right route, right outcome and right dose","Right time only, as the patient, route and drug were all correct"],"e":"Step 1: rational prescribing follows six rights: right patient, right drug, right route, right dose, right time and right outcome. Step 2: giving the drug to the wrong patient breaches right patient, IV instead of oral breaches right route, and morning instead of evening breaches right time.","w":["The drug and dose were not described as wrong.","The dose was not wrong.","The dose and outcome were not described as wrong.","The patient and route were also wrong."],"k":"Six Rights: patient, drug, route, dose, time, outcome.","rn":[["","What are the 6 Rights of rational prescribing?","What are the 6 Rights of rational prescribing?","Right patient; Right drug; Right route; Right dose; Right time; Right outcome; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":29130,"t":"Metabolism","s":"A researcher studies what happens to four drugs in the liver. Drug 1 becomes an inactive metabolite. Drug 2 becomes a less active metabolite. Drug 3 becomes a metabolite that causes liver damage. Drug 4 is inactive when given but becomes active only after metabolism.<br><br>Which statement about these outcomes is correct?","o":["All are possible metabolite outcomes; drug 4 is a pro-drug","Metabolism always inactivates drugs, so drugs 3 and 4 are impossible","Drug 4 is a toxin, not a drug","Only drug 1 is metabolised; the rest are excreted unchanged","Drug 3 is a pro-drug, because any metabolite with an effect must have been inactive before metabolism"],"e":"Step 1: metabolism chemically alters a drug to facilitate its action or enhance its elimination, and metabolites can be inactive, less active, active (with therapeutic or toxic effects) or activated (pro-drug). Step 2: a pro-drug is a precursor of the active drug that must be metabolised in vivo into the pharmacologically active form, as with drug 4.","w":["Metabolites can be active or activated as well as inactive.","A pro-drug is a drug precursor.","All four are metabolised.","Drug 3 was active before metabolism; drug 4 is the pro-drug."],"k":"Metabolites: inactive, less active, active/toxic, or activated (pro-drug).","rn":[["","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","What are the 4 possible metabolites?","Inactive; Less active; Active (therapeutic / toxic effect); Activated (pro-drug)"]]}]);
